  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM EDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nToday-Sunday...Cold front sweeps southward through the area today. \nNortherly winds will increase behind the boundary becoming breezy to \nwindy through the afternoon and into tonight. Winds speeds are \nforecast to pick up to around 15-20 mph across the interior and up \nto 20-25 mph at the coast by late afternoon and early evening, with \ngusts to 30-35 mph. Wind gusts may pick up a little higher along the \ncoast into tonight, and will need to continue to monitor for the \nneed of any Wind Advisories east of I-95. Better potential for gusts \nto 35-40 mph (potentially slightly higher along the coast), \nnecessitating the need for a Wind Advisory will occur into Sunday as \nwinds veer NE and pressure gradient tightens further between stalled \nfront to the south, strong high across the eastern U.S. and \ndeveloping low pressure offshore of the Mid-Atlantic coast. \n\nThe increasing N/NE flow will produce rough surf, large breaking \nwaves, and a high risk of life-threatening rip currents at area \nbeaches. Entering the dangerous surf is highly discouraged! Long \nperiod onshore swells will also likely contribute to moderate beach \nerosion through multiple high tide cycles this weekend into early \nnext week. A High Risk for rip currents exists for area beaches \ntoday, with a High Surf Advisory going into effect later this \nevening and continuing through the weekend as seas continue to \nbuild. \n\nScattered to numerous showers and isolated storms will develop with \nthe passing front today. Any storms will be capable of producing \noccasional to frequent lightning, gusty winds up to 40-45 mph and \nheavy downpours. Greatest rain chances (up to 80%) and potential for \nlocalized heavy rainfall will be along the southern Treasure Coast \nfrom this afternoon into tonight. As low level winds veer NE and tap \ninto lingering higher moisture (PW values 2.0-2.3\"), persistent \nonshore moving bands of showers and isolated storms may develop, \nmainly across St. Lucie and Martin counties. This can lead to quick \ntotals of 2-4\" and there exists a 1 in 10 chance of amounts in \nexcess of 6\" producing flash flooding in one or two spots, \nespecially given how saturated soils are. A Slight Risk (Level 2 of \n4) for excessive rainfall exists for the southern Treasure Coast \ntoday into tonight. As drier air builds in (PW values falling below \n1.5\"), threat for locally heavy rainfall will end into Sunday. \nHowever, the stronger low level NE winds will continue to transport\nrounds of gusty scattered showers onshore into Sunday (highest \nchance for rain along the coast). \n\nTemperatures cool behind the front, with highs ranging from the \nupper 70s near to north of Orlando and low to mid 80s south. Max \ntemps on Sunday will range from the mid 70s to low 80s. Overnight \nlows will drop into the 60s for much of the area, but may remain in \nthe 70s along the immediate coast due to the gusty onshore flow.\n\nMonday-Friday...A tight pressure gradient continues across the\narea into next week, as strong area of high pressure continues\nacross the eastern U.S. and strong low lingers off the Mid-\nAtlantic/Northeast U.S. coast. This will maintain a breezy to\nwindy N/NE flow for much of the week, continuing rough surf, \nlarge breaking waves, a high risk of life-threatening rip \ncurrents and moderate beach erosion at area beaches through at \nleast early this week. Rounds of isolated to scattered onshore \nmoving showers remain in the forecast, with drier conditions \ngenerally forecast inland. Temperatures generally remain below \nnormal, with highs in the upper 70s to low 80s and overnight lows\nin the 60s.",
